### Advisory — apply where it makes the change smaller or clearer
- [major] The live_preview block is added with `management_token: ""` (an empty literal) and the prescribed `CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN` was never added to the env destructuring block (lines 23-36). Note: I verified against the installed contentstack SDK's index.d.ts (line 123: `live_preview: { enable: boolean, host: string, management_token: string }`), so deviating from the plan's `preview_token` key is correct — the installed SDK does not declare it. However, with an empty management_token and no preview token wired from env, server-side draft retrieval through this Stack has no credential to authorize draft access, putting AC 1/2/5 (draft content shown instead of published) at risk for any fetch that does not go through the live-preview-utils URL-hash injection. (src/services/contentstack.ts:76)
  - Suggested fix: Add `CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN = ""` to the env destructuring (lines 23-36) and use it: `management_token: CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN` (or wire the preview token the SDK expects). Do not leave a hardcoded empty token.
- [major] `live_preview: { enable: true, ... }` is now unconditionally enabled on the shared `Stack` (line 91) that ALL server-side and client-side fetches flow through, including production traffic with no preview session. AC 3/4 require zero behavioral change when no preview signal is active; an always-on live_preview config risks altering request headers/params on every delivery request in production. (src/services/contentstack.ts:76)
  - Suggested fix: Gate enablement on the presence of the preview token or an explicit env flag, e.g. `enable: Boolean(CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_TOKEN)`, so the non-preview path is byte-identical to current behavior.
- [minor] `setContent((prev) => ({ ...prev, page: [updatedPage as any] }))` uses an untyped `as any` with no explanatory comment, violating the TypeScript strict criterion. The sibling subscription in [[...slug]].tsx types the same value as IContentstackPage without a cast, so the cast appears unnecessary. (src/pages/404.tsx:31)
  - Suggested fix: Drop the `as any` (the listener is already typed `(updatedPage: IContentstackPage) => void`), or if the 404 content type genuinely differs, add a comment explaining the cast.
- [minor] `stackSdk: Stack as any` has an eslint-disable but no explanation of why the cast is needed (the live-preview-utils IStackSdk type is structural and may accept the SDK Stack with a narrower assertion). An unexplained `as any` hides real type drift between the delivery SDK and live-preview-utils versions. (src/pages/_app.tsx:48)
  - Suggested fix: Replace with a documented, narrower cast (e.g. `as unknown as IStackSdk` imported from @contentstack/live-preview-utils) plus a one-line comment explaining the mismatch, or remove the cast if the types align.
- [minor] `host: CONTENTSTACK_API_HOST || "api.contentstack.io"` hardcodes the endpoint URL string. Per project convention, endpoint URLs should come from named constants. (src/services/contentstack.ts:78)
  - Suggested fix: Move the default host into a named constant (e.g. in constants/contentstack) and reference it here.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- Given the Contentstack client is mocked to signal a draft entry change for the MX Homepage, the rendered Homepage shows the draft entry content instead of the previously published content, without requiring a full page reload.
- Given the Contentstack client is mocked to signal a draft entry change for a Discover Article page, the rendered Discover Article page shows the draft entry content instead of the previously published content, without requiring a full page reload.
- When no preview signal is active, the MX Homepage renders published content identically to its current behavior with no visual or functional regression.
- When no preview signal is active, Discover Article pages render published content identically to their current behavior with no visual or functional regression.
- Given the Contentstack client is mocked to signal multiple successive draft entry changes for the same Homepage, the rendered Homepage updates to reflect each change in sequence without a full page reload.
- Given the Contentstack client is mocked to signal a draft entry change while the page is in a protected or gated state, the page does not render the draft content and continues to show the published content unchanged.

